FIELD SHIFT   /   QUESTIONNAIRE & DECISION LOGIC

Document purpose and scope boundary

This document specifies the farmer-facing questions and the complete logic that turns the answers into a validated, evidence-backed input package for rotation assessment. It covers the questionnaire frontend, conditional questions, location and environmental API retrieval, input provenance, calculations, constraints, scoring inputs, backend processing, persistence, loading/progress events, failure handling and acceptance tests.

This is not a specification of the Crop Rotation Planner results page, rotation cards, comparison page, rotation timeline, or the wider Rotation Planner interface. Those are outside this document. The only page-level handoff described here is the interface contract that passes the verified questionnaire snapshot to the existing Crop Rotation Planner route when the farmer chooses to continue.

The phrase “use APIs to fetch information” is interpreted as permission to use external data APIs after the farmer provides or confirms the relevant farm/field location and accepts the applicable data-sharing notice. The farmer's precise farm location must not be guessed from an IP address.

Source and implementation status

This specification is grounded in the supplied Field Shift v8 master plan, especially its crop-planner wizard, core and optional questions, review stage, real loading stages, scientific-integrity rules, API/job contracts, source registry and rotation-engine requirements. It expands those requirements into an implementable questionnaire and backend contract.

The master plan's retained code-audit notes say that the supplied implementation snapshot visibly fetches NASA POWER monthly climate and Open-Meteo current weather, while other satellite/data products are listed in the interface without verified active integrations. It also identifies timer-driven fake analysis progress, localStorage-based persistence and the absence of a durable job queue/database in the reviewed snapshot. Treat that as the status of the supplied audit snapshot, not proof of the live deployment today. Before implementation, inspect the current repository and run real connector smoke tests. Never show a source as connected merely because its name is mentioned in code or documentation.

This specification distinguishes:

Existing/audit-observed: explicitly identified as active in the supplied code-audit note.

Planned/needs integration: required or proposed by the product plan but not verified as active in that audit snapshot.

Farmer-provided: entered by the farmer and not independently measured.

Farmer-confirmed suggestion: a value proposed from a dataset and accepted or corrected by the farmer.

Unavailable/failed: not retrieved, not supported, or not suitable for this field/run.

No implementation may convert a planned integration into a false “live” status.

Binding Override — Simple Mode Is the Default

HIGHEST-PRIORITY QUESTIONNAIRE RULE

This section overrides any earlier wording that could be interpreted as showing the full question catalogue during normal first-time use. The detailed question catalogue in Section 4 is a reusable library of conditional and advanced questions. It is not the default onboarding checklist.

Required default experience

Launch every new questionnaire in Simple Mode. Simple is selected by default; Advanced is an explicit user choice.

The ordinary first-run path contains four short question steps at most. Saved farm/field data and already-known answers are prefilled and skipped rather than repeated.

Ask only for field identity/location, recent crop sequence, broad water access and the farmer’s main goal.

Use sensible, disclosed defaults for secondary settings. Do not ask soil chemistry, irrigation volumes, detailed management, yield, cost or risk questions before the first pass.

Allow “Not sure” for non-blocking information. Missing facts reduce coverage/confidence or make only the affected calculation unavailable; they are never replaced with ideal values.

At the end of the short flow, ask whether the farmer wants a few extra questions for greater accuracy. Continuing without extra questions must always be available.

Four-step Simple Mode flow

STEP 1 — FARM, FIELD AND LOCATION. Select an existing farm and field. If none exists, create a simple field record, enter an optional label, and confirm a location using place search or a map pin. Reuse saved location. Drawing a field polygon is optional and must not block a location-only first pass.

STEP 2 — RECENT CROPS. Ask what has grown in the field over the last three growing seasons, or the seasons the farmer remembers. Allow repeatable crop entries, approximate season/year, “I don’t remember / Not sure”, and separate Fallow and Cover Crop choices. Do not request detailed dates or yields in Simple Mode.

STEP 3 — WATER ACCESS. Ask Rain-fed / Irrigated / Both / Not sure. If Irrigated or Both and reliability materially affects candidate feasibility, ask at most one short follow-up: Reliable / Seasonal or limited / Variable / Not sure. Do not ask irrigation method, frequency, litres, flow rate or water chemistry initially.

STEP 4 — MAIN GOAL. Ask what matters most: Balanced plan (preselected default) / Save water / Improve soil / Climate resilience / Crop suitability / Productivity potential. The default is visible and editable, never hidden.

Disclosed defaults — not additional mandatory questions

Planning horizon: default to three growing seasons for a first-pass annual-crop assessment. Show it in the compact review and let the farmer change it. This is not a forced rotation length: crop duration, perennial status and feasibility still govern the engine.

Soil: use verified available evidence as a clearly labelled dataset estimate, or leave it unknown. Do not ask texture, pH, salinity, nutrients or laboratory-test questions in the initial flow.

Boundary: reuse a saved polygon where available. Otherwise a confirmed point is enough for a location-only first pass. Offer boundary drawing later as an accuracy improvement.

Crop requirements/exclusions: assume none only when the farmer accepts that disclosed default. Otherwise ask during optional refinement.

End-of-flow accuracy choice

After the four steps, show a compact summary and ask: “Would you like to improve the accuracy with a few extra details?” Provide two choices: “Use my current information and continue” (primary, always available) and “Answer a few optional questions”. Do not use warning language that makes the farmer feel the basic answers are inadequate.

If the farmer opts in, rank only follow-ups relevant to the selected field and likely to change feasibility, candidate eligibility, water assessment or ranking.

Ask one question at a time, explain why it matters, show “Skip for now” and “Finish and continue”, and let the farmer stop at any time.

Ask no more than three high-impact follow-ups in the first refinement pass. “More accuracy options” is a separate secondary route for farmers who explicitly want more detail.

Typical candidates: soil texture/test, required or excluded crops, irrigation reliability/method, known crop-specific pest/disease constraints, growing-season frequency/planting windows, and relevant management practices. Ask yield/economic questions only if a validated model actually uses them.

Do not repeat information already saved, supplied, retrieved or confirmed. Choose questions by expected decision value: ask only when the answer could materially reduce uncertainty, change a feasibility decision or alter the ranking.

Meaning of “Required” in Section 4

A catalogue item marked “Required: Yes” is required only inside the branch or calculation that invokes it. It does not make the question part of default Simple Mode. If a missing item is genuinely necessary for one calculation, ask one concise clarification with a reason or omit that calculation and explain the limitation; never force completion of the entire catalogue.

