2.3 Progress and navigation UI

Simple Mode: show only the four-step progress indicator, such as Step 2 of 4. Skip steps already completed from saved farm/field data; update the visible sequence honestly. Never show the full advanced question count or imply every farmer must answer the entire library.

Optional refinement: if the farmer chooses extra accuracy questions, extend the visible flow only with the currently selected follow-ups. The indicator must reflect the real active set and allow the farmer to finish early.

Advanced Mode: progress may be grouped into Field, Land & Soil, Water, Crop History, Planning, Priorities and Review. Calculate the active route from actual branch rules; never display a fixed total that becomes false. When a branch count is not yet known, show truthful section progress rather than a fabricated percentage.

Completed sections use a check icon plus text. Current section uses the leaf accent. Not-yet-visited sections remain neutral.

Back navigation preserves every valid answer. Browser refresh resumes the saved draft and current question.

Keyboard: Tab/Shift+Tab, arrow navigation for radios, Enter/Space for selection, Escape only where safe, visible focus ring and correct screen-reader labels.

Respect prefers-reduced-motion. Question transitions use restrained opacity/transform over roughly 180–240ms; never animate heavy blur continuously.

2.4 Answer-control patterns

Use the following control conventions consistently:

Single-select cards: one answer from mutually exclusive options, e.g. rain-fed / irrigated / both.

Multi-select chips: several independent crops, sources or constraints may be selected.

Searchable crop selector: crop names, synonyms and family appear distinctly; no ambiguous free-text crop aliases in canonical storage.

Repeatable season record: add one row per actual season/crop, each with year/date/status and optional notes.

Numeric field with unit selector: never accept a bare number where the unit changes meaning.

Range/date control: use only when the farmer knows the range/date; allow “not sure”.

Map/polygon control: pin and polygon are separate modes with separate save operations.

Lab result group: every measurement carries unit, sample date, depth and test/lab source.

Priority slider: 0–100 preference strength with a readable label; show normalized percentages on the final review.

Natural-language note: can help the farmer describe a practice, but parsed fields are a draft requiring confirmation.

2.5 Question-card content hierarchy

Every question screen should use the following sequence:

1. SECTION / STEP eyebrow.

2. One direct farmer-facing question.

3. One short explanation in everyday language.

4. Main answer control.

5. A visible unknown/skip option when optional.

6. “Why we ask” disclosure with the agronomic/data reason.

7. Optional “Use suggested value” area if a real data source has returned a candidate value.

8. Inline validation and unit/help text beside the affected field.

9. Back, Continue, Save & exit actions.

The system must not display an API-sourced answer as though the farmer typed it. Show source labels such as Suggested from mapped soil data, Weather model estimate, You entered this, or Lab result supplied by you.

