The v8 plan lists these as potential categories, not proof that they are all active. Provider status must be checked during deployment and recorded in the source registry.

6.3 Specific API request patterns

These examples are engineering references, not hardcoded UI calls. Server adapters should construct requests, apply timeouts, validate response shape and normalize the values.

Place-name geocoding: Open-Meteo documents https://geocoding-api.open-meteo.com/v1/search?name={query}&count={n}&language=en. The response can include WGS84 latitude/longitude, country/admin areas, timezone and elevation. Show the ranked results and require farmer selection; never auto-pick the first result if ambiguous.

Current/forecast conditions: Open-Meteo documents https://api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast with latitude, longitude, requested current, hourly and daily variables and timezone. Request only variables needed by the question/calculation. Current and forecast data are model outputs with validity timestamps, not farmer measurements.

Historical weather: Open-Meteo documents the /v1/archive endpoint for a coordinate/time range. The response must retain the dataset/model and temporal resolution. Reanalysis must not be called a local weather-station reading.

NASA POWER Daily: use the documented Daily point API with latitude, longitude, start, end, parameters, community, time-standard and format. NASA POWER notes that Daily results are by day and that time standard defaults to Local Solar Time unless otherwise specified. The adapter should request and store a consistent explicit time standard, preferably UTC for canonical daily aggregation with local display conversion handled separately.

Important: never request an entire global catalogue for one field. Request only the point/grid cell and date range needed, cache by provider/product/variable/location cell/date window/version and respect provider rate limits and terms.

6.4 Data retrieval sequence

1. Validate the location point, polygon version and consent record.

2. Resolve timezone and optional elevation; preserve provider response metadata.

3. Determine spatial sampling method based on field geometry and each dataset's native resolution.

4. Ask the source registry which providers are configured, operational and appropriate for the requested variables.

5. Fetch current/short-range forecast only for context; do not treat a 7–16 day forecast as a multi-year rotation climate baseline.

6. Fetch sufficient historical daily/monthly data for the actual calculation. Never combine monthly climatology with a daily metric as though they had the same temporal resolution.

7. Fetch optional soil/terrain/vegetation products only if their connectors are verified and their resolution is appropriate.

8. Normalize dates, timezone, units, coordinate reference system, missing-value codes and quality flags.

9. Compare sources where useful. Do not blindly average conflicting datasets; select a documented primary source and record disagreements.

10. Save raw response references or reproducible source metadata, canonical observations and retrieval timestamps.

11. Produce a source-coverage report and identify the calculations that are still valid, degraded or unavailable.

6.5 Weather and climate feature extraction

Only compute a feature when the source contains the required variables and the date range is suitable.

Daily minimum, maximum and mean temperature, each with units and time basis.

Daily and seasonal precipitation sums, with period boundaries and source type.

Solar radiation/shortwave radiation where available.

Humidity, dew point and vapour-pressure deficit only when required inputs exist.

Growing Degree Days (GDD) with a crop-specific base and any cap/floor sourced from the crop knowledge record. A common daily form is max(0, ((Tmax + Tmin) / 2) - Tbase), but the selected crop method must define whether upper/lower caps are applied and which days are in the accumulation period.

Heat-stress days only with a crop-specific threshold and known sensitive stage/time window.

Frost exposure only with a supported temperature threshold and planting-stage relevance.

Dry-spell length from a documented daily-rainfall threshold and minimum consecutive days.

Rainfall anomaly only against a comparable historical baseline with a stated period and sample size.

Dry / typical / wet seasons only from documented historical quantiles or year-selection criteria; historical cases are not forecasts.

Do not compute a trend from a short series and present it as a long-term climate trend. “Not enough history to assess” is a valid result.

6.6 Soil, water and vegetation retrieval cautions

A mapped soil property is a prediction/prior for a grid cell, not a lab sample from the field.

A SMAP model-assisted moisture estimate is not a direct measurement from the exact field.

Vegetation indices must come from valid imagery/product processing. NDVI must never be synthesized from precipitation.

Satellite pixels must be screened for clouds/quality and the valid-pixel proportion must be shown.

Terrain slope may support a contextual risk feature; slope alone does not establish erosion, runoff or drainage status.

If provider resolution is larger than the field, label it regional/grid context, not exact field observation.

Preserve the distinction between observed, modelled, forecast, climatological, farmer-reported and derived values.

7. Canonical answer and evidence data model

The questionnaire should write a normalized snapshot, not a collection of untyped strings.

7.1 Answer object

Every answer should contain, at minimum:

answerId, questionId, questionVersion;

farmId, fieldId, questionnaireId, profileVersion;

typed value (string, number, boolean, enum, array, date, geometry or object);

unit where applicable;

scope (farm, field, subfield/zone or season/crop occurrence);

sourceType (farmer_reported, farmer_confirmed, dataset_estimate, lab_result, derived, ai_interpreted_pending_confirmation, unknown, not_applicable, skipped);

sourceName, sourceProduct, sourceUrl or reference if external;

validTime / observation date, retrievedAt, confirmedAt;

measurement depth / spatial basis / crop stage where applicable;

quality flags, confidence metadata and notes;

createdAt, updatedAt, createdBy and version.

Do not encode several states into a single free-form label and then guess the status in the UI.

7.2 Separate canonical records

Use different records for:

Farmer answer: what the farmer says about their field.

Dataset observation: what an external provider returned.

Farmer-confirmed estimate: a dataset suggestion that the farmer reviewed and accepted.

Management routine: what normally happens, e.g. irrigation every three days.

Management event: what actually happened on a date, e.g. irrigation applied today.

Crop history occurrence: what was actually planted/harvested in a past or current season.

Rotation candidate/plan: a proposed sequence; not a historical or active crop occurrence until deliberately selected/recorded.

7.3 Draft and snapshot lifecycle

Autosave each valid answer to persistent storage; show Draft saved only after successful write acknowledgement.

Save current questionId, branch context and schema version so a browser refresh resumes correctly.

On failed save, keep a local recoverable draft and show Not yet saved; do not claim success.

The final review creates an immutable input snapshot/version. Any later change creates a new version and marks downstream outputs stale.

Old reports must retain the exact inputs and source data used at the time; new retrieval does not rewrite an old run.

8. Calculations and decision logic that consume the answers

This section defines the calculation contract, not the design of the rotation results page. Every calculation must be a pure, versioned engine function with typed inputs/outputs and unit tests.

8.1 Input normalization and validation

Before any crop scoring:

1. Convert units to canonical units while retaining original value/unit.

2. Validate ranges and physical plausibility without silently clamping user input.

3. Resolve synonyms to stable crop IDs and crop-family IDs.

4. Convert date ranges into timezone-aware season records; preserve approximate vs exact dates.

5. Ensure each input has scope: farm vs field vs season vs event.

6. Resolve geometry and compute area only with a suitable geodesic/projected method.

7. Attach provenance and quality flags to every observation.

8. Determine which metrics are computable, degraded or unavailable.

A schema error blocks the invalid input and asks for correction. A missing optional input does not become a default “good” input.

8.2 Crop-knowledge record prerequisites

The crop library must be typed, versioned and source-tagged. A crop record should hold only supported attributes, such as:

crop identity, synonyms, annual/perennial behavior and crop family;

planting/harvest or growth-duration ranges by appropriate climate/region context;

temperature/GDD thresholds, heat/frost sensitivity and stage relevance;

water-demand parameters and growth-stage crop coefficients where sourced;

soil texture, pH, salinity and drainage tolerance ranges where sourced;

rooting depth / water extraction parameters where supported;

nutrient removal/contribution and nitrogen fixation assumptions where supported;

crop-host/pathogen relationships and family return intervals where supported;

parameter source, confidence/quality and version.

An unknown crop property remains unknown. Do not create a complete agronomic profile based only on the crop's name.

8.3 Water calculations

Keep the following variables distinct:

ET0: reference evapotranspiration, method/source/period required;

Kc: crop coefficient for a sourced crop and growth stage;

ETc: crop evapotranspiration estimate, e.g. ETc = Kc × ET0 where prerequisites hold;

P: observed/reanalysis/forecast precipitation with type and time range;

Peff: effective rainfall estimated by a documented method, not equal to raw precipitation by definition;

I: actual farmer-confirmed irrigation event/amount;

Iroutine: expected management routine, not actual water applied;

runoff/drainage and root-zone storage terms only when supported.

A simplified storage balance may be represented as S_t = S_(t-1) + Peff + I - ETc - runoff - drainage, but this equation is usable only if all included terms, the time step, sign convention, soil-water capacity and initial condition are defined. Do not silently omit unknown terms and then present the result as a fully measured water balance.

If an interval-only routine is known but its amount is not, the engine can use it as a qualitative management context; it cannot derive applied millimetres. If soil available-water capacity/rooting depth is unknown, do not claim precise plant-available water, readily available water or root-zone stress.

Use FAO-56 Penman–Monteith only if its required meteorological inputs, unit conventions and calculations are actually implemented and validated. A temperature-only heuristic must be labelled a simple estimate, not FAO-56. If using a provider's ET0 variable, retain the provider's method/product metadata and verify the exact meaning before treating it as a reference value.

8.4 Crop suitability calculation

Crop suitability is a single-crop / field / planting-window fit. It is not the same as the sequence score.

Possible factors when available:

temperature and growing-season length;

GDD and crop growth duration;

heat/frost exposure at relevant stages;

rainfall and water availability;

soil texture and pH;

salinity and drainage;

slope/terrain where justified;

planting/harvest-window feasibility.

Normalize each supported factor to a documented 0–1 scale and use sourced crop-specific response curves/ranges. The v8 plan specifies a documented weighted-geometric approach and a limiting-factor guard. One implementation form is:

Suitability = 100 × exp( Σ_i w_i × ln(max(ε, f_i)) )

where f_i is a supported factor score, w_i is a normalized factor weight, and ε avoids taking ln(0) while preserving a near-zero penalty. The exact ε, factor curves, weights and limiting thresholds must be versioned and validated; they must not be chosen just to create attractive scores.

Before scoring, apply hard minimum rules. If a crop crosses a verified non-negotiable temperature, soil, salinity, water, duration or planting-window limit, mark the crop infeasible or apply the explicitly documented cap. The farmer's preference sliders cannot undo this.

Missing factors are not assigned 1.0 or a neutral ideal. Track available-factor coverage and confidence separately. Where the factor is safety/feasibility-critical, withhold a positive suitability claim until sufficient evidence exists. Where it is optional, a provisional score may be calculated from supported factors only if the method explicitly accounts for missing weight and clearly labels the result provisional.

8.5 Rotation-sequence constraints and score inputs

A multi-season sequence must be evaluated after single-crop suitability and hard-feasibility checks.

Hard constraints (feasibility gate)

user-confirmed crop exclusions;

user-required crops, if compatible with the horizon and all hard rules;

crop-family return intervals and maximum repeated-host exposure where crop knowledge supports them;

disease-host restrictions only where the crop/host relationship is verified;

planting/harvest transition feasibility and growth duration;

climate and season compatibility;

known soil/water limits and crop tolerance floors;

perennial or multi-year occupancy rules;

number of crops/seasons per year and field availability;

horizon and timing feasibility.

If no candidate survives, return no_feasible_candidate with the exact rule(s) causing rejection. Do not force an invalid sequence to fill the UI.

Weighted preferences (ranking only)

Candidate components may include supported suitability, water demand/efficiency, soil trajectory, climate resilience, productivity potential and crop diversity. Each component must have a registered definition, direction, units, inputs, normalization, version and limitations. Preferences alter component weights; they must not alter raw weather or soil observations.

For a custom priority vector, normalize non-negative preference values using w_j = p_j / Σp_j. Keep the preset mapping versioned. The actual composite aggregation method must be documented and calibrated before displaying a 0–100 match score. Do not imply the score is a yield percentage or success probability.

Sequence carry-over

A previous crop may affect the next crop through a soil-water state, residue, nitrogen contribution, rooting profile, crop-family interval or host-risk rule. Carry-over is included only where the model has sourced parameters and tested assumptions. A legume nitrogen credit must appear in the correct following crop and account for documented decay/availability; never award a blanket universal nitrogen bonus.

Fallow and Cover Crop are separate sequence choices. Their consequences depend on water recharge, erosion cover, biomass, weeds, nutrient effects, season and crop/soil data. Neither is inherently best.

8.6 Candidate-generation method

Use a bounded deterministic search method such as beam search or dynamic programming where appropriate.

Use stable candidate/rotation IDs; do not rely on display names as IDs.

Use normalized input hash + engine version + crop-library version as the reproducibility seed/context.

Apply feasibility constraints during expansion where possible, not only after generating a large invalid result set.

Preserve rejected-candidate reasons for audit and explainability.

Return diverse candidates; do not show many near-identical sequences just to fill a list.

Record the intermediate calculation trace: eligible crop set, excluded choices and reasons, transition checks, component values and tie-break decisions.

8.7 Dry / typical / wet stress testing

Where a sufficiently long historical annual/seasonal series exists, choose dry, typical and wet cases using documented quantiles or selection criteria. Apply the same crop/water model and assumptions to each case. Store selected years/sample count, demand, available rainfall/irrigation, any deficit indicator, uncertainty and source.

These are historical stress-test scenarios, not weather forecasts. If the historical series is too short, incomplete or temporally incompatible, mark the section unavailable or limited; do not manufacture a distribution.

8.8 Confidence and missing evidence

Keep separate concepts:

Match/suitability: fit under the current inputs and preferences.

Evidence coverage: which required/supporting inputs and sources are present.

Evidence quality: freshness, source quality, spatial resolution, measurement method and uncertainty.

Confidence: an interpretation of how strongly the evidence supports the result, only shown as a numeric score if a defined and validated method exists.

Until a confidence formula has been validated, use clear labels such as Strong evidence, Some gaps, Limited evidence with reasons and a coverage list, rather than an invented percentage. Never let one composite figure conceal a missing soil test or a coarse regional grid.

9. Final review before analysis

The review screen is the farmer's last chance to correct the snapshot before it is used. In Simple Mode, show a compact summary of the four core answers, the disclosed horizon default, retrieved evidence and material limitations. Keep unasked advanced fields collapsed; do not render empty rows for the entire question library.

9.1 Review groups

In Simple Mode, display only groups that have answers or materially relevant evidence. Advanced Mode may reveal the full groups below:

1. Farm & Field

2. Location & Boundary

3. Soil & Land

4. Water & Irrigation

5. Crop History & Current Crop

6. Planning Horizon

7. Crop Requirements & Exclusions

8. Management Practices

9. Priorities & Risk

10. External Data Retrieved

11. Missing Information & Limitations

9.2 Required row fields

Every answer row shows:

question/attribute label;

current value and unit;

field/season scope;

origin/source badge: You provided, Farmer-confirmed, Dataset estimate, Lab result, Derived, Skipped, Unknown, AI-interpreted — needs confirmation;

observation/sample date and retrieval/confirmation date where applicable;

Edit action;

warning if the value is stale, invalid, approximate or spatially coarse.

9.3 Missing-data summary

Group gaps by impact:

Blocks calculation: invalid or missing input required for a hard feasibility decision or required consent.

Reduces coverage: useful optional input absent, but a first-pass assessment can continue.

Not applicable: the question does not apply to this field system.

Source unavailable: provider did not return data or integration is not active.

Needs farmer confirmation: auto-suggested value has not been accepted.

Do not use alarming language for a normal unknown. Explain what decision it limits and how the farmer could add the information later.

9.4 Explicit continue choice

The farmer chooses one of:

Save profile and exit;

Save draft;

Use current information and continue (passes the immutable Simple Mode questionnaire/evidence snapshot reference).

Answer a few optional questions (opens only the highest-value conditional follow-ups, then returns to the compact review).

The route handoff must pass stable farmId, fieldId, questionnaireId, inputSnapshotId, inputVersion, sourceCoverageId and analysisRequestId references. The Crop Rotation Planner route owns its subsequent workflow. This document does not define that route's content or layout.

10. Live analysis loading screen and truthful activity log

The loading screen is a major trust feature. It must show the work being performed in real time. It must not simulate a fixed countdown, invent provider calls, or pretend that a model is thinking when the backend has not emitted an event.

10.1 Visual composition

Full-page #07130C canvas.

Central glass panel with --fs-glass-strong or --fs-glass-default, 16px radius, 14px backdrop blur and 12% subtle border.

At top: field name, farm, request/job ID in Advanced Mode, current stage title and elapsed timer.

Beneath: a compact stage rail showing pending, active, completed, partial and failed states with text/icon, not colour alone.

Main region: an expanding activity tree/timeline. Each new task is inserted as a child beneath its owning stage; as more operations happen, the scrollable activity region grows downward. Keep the newest active event visible without forcing the whole page to jump.

Right-side/expandable Sources & data area lists only providers actually contacted or cache entries actually used, their status and how they affect coverage.

A What this means line explains the current operation in everyday language.

Bottom actions: Cancel analysis when cancellation is supported; Continue in background only if the backend job genuinely persists and the UI can reopen it.

10.2 What the timer says

When a stage starts, store a backend startedAt timestamp. The displayed timer counts elapsed time since that timestamp (e.g. Validating field data · 00:01, 00:02, 00:03). When the stage-complete event arrives, freeze that duration and append a completed row such as Field and input validation completed · 4.2 seconds.

The next stage starts its own timer. The previous duration remains visible as a completed activity. At the end, show the actual recorded duration for each stage and total run time.

Do not make a front-end timer decide when a stage completes. The frontend may render a one-second ticking elapsed display from the backend's startedAt, but status changes only arrive from the job system.

10.3 Activity wording: operations, not hidden chain-of-thought

It is acceptable to tell the farmer that the system is validating inputs, fetching weather, checking crop constraints or evaluating candidate sequences. Do not expose private internal chain-of-thought or fabricate an LLM's hidden reasoning as a transcript. Use concise, auditable work summaries such as:

Checking the field location and saved profile;

Fetching current weather from Open-Meteo;

Retrieving historical climate data from NASA POWER;

Checking whether the field has enough valid soil evidence;

Calculating growing-degree-day features;

Estimating water demand from supported weather and crop inputs;

Checking crop-family return intervals;

Testing planting-window feasibility;

Evaluating 84 candidate sequences (only if the engine actually evaluated 84);

Comparing water and soil trade-offs;

Preparing the evidence summary;

Saving the verified input and calculation snapshot.

Avoid the raw phrase Thought for 20 seconds as though hidden thoughts are being exposed. If an AI text-generation call is made, label the operation Preparing the farmer-facing explanation and report its real elapsed duration. The activity log should show observable operations and concise rationales, not private reasoning tokens.

10.4 Hierarchical event example

The activity panel may grow like this (illustrative labels only; actual entries depend on the real run):

