This is a shape example only. It must never be seeded as fixed progress. Source names, counts, status and duration are populated from real events and telemetry.

10.5 Stage lifecycle

Supported stage states:

pending — not started;

running — backend stage is active;

complete — stage completed successfully;

partial — stage completed with a known limitation;

skipped — stage was not required or source not configured;

failed — stage failed;

cancelled — cancellation was acknowledged;

retrying — transient failure is being retried.

The overall job state is one of queued, running, complete, partial, failed, retrying, cancelled or skipped. A completed job may contain a partial optional source, but a required calculation failure cannot be labelled complete.

10.6 Honest progress and percentage rules

Use named stages and real child operations as the primary progress indicator.

Do not show 73% complete unless stage weights are defensible and actual stage work can be measured.

Do not predict remaining time with fake precision.

If a provider responds from cache, display Using cached data from [provider] and the age of the cache, not Fetched from provider.

Count network calls only when calls actually occur. If two sources were contacted, show two. If one failed, show its failure; do not suppress it.

If a provider is not configured, show Not connected / not requested, not a fake zero-second success.

If an optional source fails, continue only when the remaining evidence supports a useful partial result.

10.7 Event delivery

Use Server-Sent Events (SSE) for one-way live job updates where appropriate; documented polling is an acceptable fallback. The job must remain valid if the browser refreshes or disconnects. On reconnect, request events from the last event ID or fetch the full current job snapshot and append missed events.

The backend should emit structured progress events containing:

jobId, eventId, stageKey, parentEventId;

safe display label and optional explanatory sentence;

state, startedAt, completedAt, durationMs;

sourceId or calculation key when relevant;

attempt number, cache status and retry metadata;

safe error code and farmer-safe message if needed;

timestamp and correlation ID.

Never include API keys, private prompts, stack traces or unnecessary exact coordinates in user-visible events.

11. Backend architecture and API contracts

The questionnaire UI must be thin. It stores answers and renders state; server-side services own canonical validation, source retrieval, job execution, agronomic calculations, provenance and persistence.

11.1 Required backend modules

1. Questionnaire schema service: serves versioned questions, answer options, branch rules and validation schemas.

2. Draft/profile service: creates, loads, autosaves and versions farm/field questionnaire answers.

3. Location service: geocoding adapter, timezone/elevation resolution and polygon validation/area calculation.

4. Consent/privacy service: records explicit remote-data consent and the scope of information permitted to leave local storage.

5. Connector registry: configured providers, real health state, capabilities, supported variables and terms/attribution metadata.

6. Provider adapters: Open-Meteo, NASA POWER and optional soil/vegetation/terrain connectors; each maps to a canonical observation format.

7. Data-quality service: unit/date normalization, missing-value handling, range checks, quality flags and spatial/temporal coverage.

8. Climate feature service: GDD, rainfall totals, heat/frost exposure and dry-spell metrics where supportable.

9. Water feature service: ET0/ETc and water-balance features only when required inputs exist.

10. Crop knowledge service: versioned crop parameters, crop families, disease-host rules and references.

11. Rotation assessment engine: deterministic feasibility, candidate search, scoring, trade-off and uncertainty calculations.

12. Job orchestration service: queue, retries, cancellation, stage events, idempotency and result status.

13. Snapshot/report service: immutable input/evidence/calculation records and reproducible IDs.

14. Audit/observability service: correlation IDs, durations, source outcomes and protected logs.

