}

The server must load the referenced snapshot and verify it belongs to the authenticated user. Do not trust arbitrary userId, farm ID, field ID or coordinates sent by the client without authorization and ownership checks.

11.4 Job and event persistence

Persist at least:

job ID, user/scope, questionnaire/snapshot IDs, created/started/completed times;

overall state, current stage, attempt count and idempotency key;

each stage/event with parent relationship, state, start/end/duration and safe details;

source call records (provider/product/variable/date range/cache status/latency/status/error code);

calculation/model versions and candidate search metadata;

input/evidence snapshot reference and result/report ID;

cancellation/retry information.

The current audit snapshot's localStorage and timer-only progress are not sufficient for durable jobs. Use persistent storage and a durable worker/queue (or an equivalent job runner) for work that must survive browser closure. Local-only drafts can remain supported, but the user must know when the assessment itself requires server processing and remote data retrieval.

11.5 Retry, timeout and duplicate protection

Set connection/read timeouts per provider and per stage.

Retry only transient network/server/rate-limit failures with bounded exponential backoff and jitter.

Do not retry invalid requests, unauthorized calls or unavailable products indefinitely.

Honor provider rate limits and cache by source, native grid cell, variable/product, time window and version.

Idempotency key prevents duplicate jobs when the farmer double-clicks, refreshes or retries after connection loss.

Cancellation records whether work stopped or the result was simply ignored by the client.

A repeated Run request for the same active job should reopen that job instead of spawning duplicates.

11.6 Privacy and security

Remote data retrieval is opt-in and the consent copy identifies what leaves the device (coordinates/geometry where needed, field inputs, selected management context and source request metadata).

Do not transmit the full farm profile if the provider only needs coordinates and dates.

API credentials/secrets remain server-side.

Do not log exact private farm coordinates, raw farmer notes, credentials or private AI prompt content unless specifically necessary, protected and retention-limited.

Enforce user/farm/field ownership on every read/write/job/event endpoint.

Revoke consent by stopping future remote jobs and applying the stated retention/deletion policy to remote snapshots.

12. Error handling and partial-success behavior

The questionnaire and analysis must remain understandable when data is incomplete.

12.1 Error classes

Invalid answer / unit / geometry;

location not found or ambiguous;

browser location permission denied;

external data source unavailable or rate-limited;

source response invalid or missing variable;

source too coarse or too stale for the metric;

crop knowledge parameter missing;

required agronomic constraint cannot be evaluated;

no candidate sequence passes hard constraints;

persistence/job failure;

consent revoked or authorization denied;

user cancellation.

12.2 Farmer-facing behavior

Keep the answer draft and identify the exact field to fix.

Offer a retry only when the error is retryable.

Preserve the last valid saved profile when a new save fails.

Preserve the prior successful assessment when a refresh fails.

A failed optional source may produce a partial assessment; name the affected source and metrics.

A required source/calculation failure blocks only the dependent result, not unrelated questionnaire answers.

If no candidate meets hard constraints, state that no feasible plan was found under current confirmed inputs and list the constraints that caused it. Do not force a recommendation.

12.3 Partial assessment record

A partial result must store:

successful sources and variables;

failed/unavailable/skipped sources and reasons;

calculations that ran and their input coverage;

calculations not run and the missing prerequisite;

confidence/coverage limitations;

whether the result is safe to use as a provisional assessment or must be blocked.

13. Cross-page linkage (handoff only; not page design)

The questionnaire belongs to the Crop Rotation Planner workflow. Its job ends when the farmer saves the profile or confirms the input snapshot and chooses to continue.

The handoff payload must preserve:

farmId and fieldId;

questionnaireId and questionnaireSchemaVersion;

inputSnapshotId and inputVersion;

geometryVersion and locationVersion;

sourceCoverageId and observation snapshot references;

cropKnowledgeVersion and calculation-engine version requested;

consentId and data policy;

the farmer's priority vector and confirmed constraints.

The downstream Rotation Planner must consume this immutable snapshot and must not quietly re-read a different field or replace it with another farm's answers. If the farmer changes an input later, create a new input version and mark dependent results stale. The downstream route's UI, cards, scoring display, timeline and comparison experience are explicitly outside the scope of this document.

14. Testing and acceptance criteria

No questionnaire or backend stage is complete merely because its visual control renders.

14.1 Question-flow tests

Create mode produces unique farm/field/questionnaire IDs and does not inherit another field's history.

Edit mode reloads the correct field and preserves its old snapshot.

Back navigation preserves answers and branch selections.

Refresh resumes at the correct question with saved answers.

Optional unknowns/skips do not block review.

Required missing answers route to the first missing step.

Branch changes add/remove only the relevant questions and update progress honestly.

Farm-level practices are not copied into fields without confirmation.

Current crop is never inferred from a candidate rotation.

Fallow and cover crop remain different values.

All numeric values require compatible units and reject malformed input without silent clamping.

An invalid polygon blocks geometry save but need not block unrelated profile draft save if geometry is optional.

Save failures do not show a false Draft saved message.

A first-time user starts in Simple Mode and sees no more than four core steps before the optional accuracy choice.

Existing farm/field data is prefilled and skipped rather than asked again.

Declining optional accuracy questions still allows the first-pass assessment to continue.

The first optional refinement pass contains no more than three high-impact follow-ups and can be stopped or skipped.

Simple Mode progress reflects only the active simple flow (for example, Step 2 of 4); it never exposes the full advanced question count.

14.2 Location/data tests

Geocoding ambiguity requires farmer selection.

Browser location permission denial offers manual search/pin.

IP location is never accepted as confirmed farm location.

Location point and field polygon are stored separately.

Polygon area is not computed in unprojected degrees.

Coarse data grids are not presented as field-level measurement when field size/resolution do not support that claim.

NASA POWER monthly output is not treated as daily history.

Open-Meteo current/forecast is distinct from historical reanalysis.

Source labels match the actual provider/product requested.

A planned or unavailable provider never displays Connected.

Cache hits are not counted as live network fetches.

API failures and source quality flags are visible and persisted.

14.3 Scientific calculation tests

GDD uses documented crop thresholds/caps and the correct daily series.

ET0 method is named correctly; no FAO-56 claim for a simplified proxy.

ETc requires supported Kc/stage and valid ET0 inputs.

Effective rainfall is not automatically equal to raw precipitation.

Irrigation routine is not treated as actual irrigation event.

Water balance requires declared time step, units, storage basis and included terms.

Unknown soil properties are not assigned ideal values.

Mapped soil estimates remain distinct from lab results.

Crop-family/disease constraints use verified crop knowledge only.

Hard constraints execute before weighted ranking.

Preference changes alter ranking weights, not weather/soil observations or feasibility.

Same normalized input + engine/crop-library versions reproduce the same candidate ranking.

Candidate set is diverse and deterministic.

No feasible candidate returns explicit reasons rather than a fabricated plan.

Score is not labelled as yield chance or probability.

Confidence remains separate from match.

Dry/typical/wet stress tests use documented historical quantiles and never pretend to be forecasts.

14.4 Loading/progress tests

The active timer starts from a real backend startedAt event.

Completed stage duration freezes on the real completion event.

Stage text changes only on backend state updates.

Nested child events appear under the correct parent stage.

Source labels/counts exactly match actual provider requests/cache use.

Failed/partial/skipped/retrying/cancelled events display correctly.

Browser refresh/reconnect restores job state and missed events.

Duplicate submit does not create duplicate jobs.

No fixed fake percentage or made-up “thought for” duration appears.

Job logs contain no secrets or unnecessary private location data.

14.5 Backend and security tests

Request/response DTOs validate on the server.

Every endpoint enforces user/farm/field authorization.

Job creation is idempotent.

Transient failures retry within configured bounds; permanent errors do not loop.

Worker restart preserves queued/running job state safely.

Cancellation and consent revocation have explicit, testable outcomes.

Concurrent edits use version/ETag conflict handling rather than silent overwrite.

Immutable snapshots remain unchanged after profile edits or source refresh.

14.6 UI and accessibility tests

Validate at 1600, 1440, 1280, 1024, 768, 430, 390 and 360 CSS px. Check:

question text, labels, units and errors are not clipped;

no content overlaps and no horizontal page overflow occurs;

glass surfaces maintain sufficient contrast over actual backgrounds;

backdrop-filter fallback remains legible;

touch targets are at least 44×44px;

keyboard focus is visible and control names are accessible;

screen reader announces question, required/optional state, validation and save status;

reduced-motion preference works;

selection is never conveyed by colour alone.

15. Implementation order and completion gates

Phase 1 — Questionnaire correctness

Implement the versioned question schema and branch engine.

Implement create/edit draft flows, per-field profiles and autosave.

Implement location point and field polygon as separate records.

Implement farmer-provided data provenance and review.

Implement custom/preset priorities and explicit unknown states.

Gate: all question-flow tests pass; draft refresh and branch changes preserve correct answers.

Phase 2 — Real location and weather integrations

Implement server-side geocoding adapter and confirmed location flow.

Verify current Open-Meteo connector and add explicit forecast/historical calls as needed.

Upgrade/verify NASA POWER daily retrieval where needed; do not assume monthly climate covers daily calculations.

Add provider registry/coverage UI and source-quality metadata.

Gate: live smoke tests prove actual requests and source metadata; current vs forecast vs historical series are separated.

Phase 3 — Optional soil, water and satellite evidence

Add only verified soil/terrain/moisture/vegetation connectors.

Record native resolution, time/depth semantics, quality flags and uncertainty.

Handle paused/unavailable providers honestly.

Add water calculations only after ET and management input prerequisites are validated.

Gate: each metric has a registered definition and test; no source is displayed as active without a verified call.

Phase 4 — Deterministic assessment and auditable progress

Implement typed crop knowledge and pure calculation functions.

Implement hard constraints, normalized preferences, deterministic candidate search and intermediate traces.

Implement durable job orchestration, event persistence, SSE/polling and accurate elapsed timers.

Persist immutable snapshots and source/calc versions.

Gate: same inputs reproduce; job events match real work; partial/failure handling works.

Phase 5 — Quality and production readiness

Run visual viewport checks and accessibility tests.

Run build, type-check, unit, integration, end-to-end and API smoke tests.

Test privacy consent, ownership, retries, cancellation, retention and logs.

Produce a completion report listing complete, partial, blocked and unverified items.

Gate: do not claim completion until the real target environment passes the build and tests. A beautiful questionnaire with simulated evidence or fake progress is not complete.

16. Non-negotiable “never do this” list

1. Never use IP geolocation as a confirmed field location.

2. Never confuse a location pin with a field boundary.

3. Never present coarse regional/grid data as an exact field measurement.

4. Never label a mapped soil prediction as a lab test.

5. Never call reanalysis a local weather-station observation.

6. Never call a monthly climate average daily weather history.

7. Never manufacture NDVI or soil moisture from unrelated variables.

8. Never use an API name/badge as proof the integration is live.

9. Never claim a forecast was retrieved if only historical data was fetched.

10. Never treat an irrigation routine as proof of actual water applied.

11. Never infer that a recommended crop is currently planted.

12. Never merge fallow and cover crop into one category.

13. Never turn unknown values into ideal values.

14. Never let preference weights bypass hard agronomic feasibility.

15. Never call a match score a yield percentage or success probability.

16. Never combine confidence and suitability into one unexplained score.

17. Never claim FAO-56 when the required-input method is not implemented.

18. Never fabricate candidate counts, source counts, progress percentages or elapsed stage durations.

19. Never show hidden chain-of-thought as a transcript; show real operations and concise rationale summaries.

20. Never mark a job complete before the calculation and snapshot persistence have actually completed.

21. Never overwrite an immutable historical input/source snapshot.

22. Never display “saved”, “connected”, “complete” or “deleted” before the corresponding operation is acknowledged.

17. Official technical references

The product's v8 master plan remains the primary source for Field Shift-specific requirements. The following public documentation is included to guide live connector implementation and must be rechecked at implementation/deployment time for endpoint changes, terms, parameter availability and rate limits.

